# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The man and the first son (21:28–29). “But what think ye? A certain man had two sons; and he came to the first, and said, Son, go work to day in my vineyard. He answered and said, I will not: but afterward he repented, and went.” A man has two sons. He comes to the first and tells him to go work that day in his vineyard. The first son answers, “I will not.” “Afterward” he repented, and he went.'))
A(('p', '3.2 What the first son’s story says and does not say. The text says the command was for “to day” and that the answer was a plain refusal. It says the son “repented” and “went.” It does not say what moved him, how long afterward it was, what work he did in the vineyard, or what the father said to him on his return. The story reports the refusal, the change and the going.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The second son (21:30). “And he came to the second, and said likewise. And he answered and said, I go, sir: and went not.” The father gives the second son the same command. The second son answers “I go, sir,” and does not go. The text does not say why he did not go, and it does not say whether the father learned of it.'))
A(('p', '3.4 Jesus’ question (21:31). “Whether of them twain did the will of his father? They say unto him, The first.” The story ends with a question, and the question is about doing the father’s will. The answer is given by the men to whom Jesus speaks: “The first.”'))
A(('p', '3.5 The contrasts. The two sons share one command and one father. They differ in two things the text shows: what each said, and what each did. The first said no and went. The second said yes and did not go. Jesus asks which of them did the will of his father, and the question is about the deed.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 21:28', '“A certain man had two sons”', 'A man, two sons.'],
    ['Matt 21:28', '“Son, go work to day in my vineyard”', 'The command, given to the first.'],
    ['Matt 21:29', '“I will not: but afterward he repented, and went”', 'Refusal, then repentance, then going.'],
    ['Matt 21:30', '“I go, sir: and went not”', 'Agreement in word, no going.'],
    ['Matt 21:31', '“Whether of them twain did the will of his father?”', 'Jesus’ question.'],
    ['Matt 21:31', '“They say unto him, The first.”', 'The answer given to Him.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.6 What the story leaves unsaid. The story does not say who the father is. It does not say what the vineyard is, or who the first or the second son is. It does not say what the work is, and it does not say what became of either son after the going and the not going. It gives the command, the two answers, the two deeds and the question, and it stops.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives an application, not a list of identifications. In Matthew 13 Jesus explains the Sower and the Wheat and the Weeds part by part. Here He does not. He asks one question about the story (21:31), receives the answer, and then applies the answer to two groups of people. This section records His words exactly.'))
A(('p', '4.2 Jesus’ question. “Whether of them twain did the will of his father?” (21:31). The answer the men give is, “The first” (21:31).'))
A(('p', '4.3 Jesus’ application. “Jesus saith unto them, Verily I say unto you, That the publicans and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before you. For John came unto you in the way of righteousness, and ye believed him not: but the publicans and the harlots believed him: and ye, when ye had seen it, repented not afterward, that ye might believe him” (21:31–32). Jesus speaks these words to those who answered Him, “unto them” (21:31), and says “you” to them.'))
A(('ctbl', 'What Jesus states in 21:31–32 is set out below, in His own words.', [
    ['What Jesus says', 'His words', 'Verse'],
    ['Who go into the kingdom of God', '“the publicans and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before you”', 'Matt 21:31'],
    ['How John came', '“John came unto you in the way of righteousness”', 'Matt 21:32'],
    ['What “ye” did', '“ye believed him not”', 'Matt 21:32'],
    ['What the publicans and the harlots did', '“the publicans and the harlots believed him”', 'Matt 21:32'],
    ['What “ye” did afterward', '“ye, when ye had seen it, repented not afterward, that ye might believe him”', 'Matt 21:32'],
], [0.30, 0.54, 0.16]))
A(('p', 'These are the only identifications Jesus gives, and they concern two groups of people and what each did about John. The groups are set against the answer to His question, and the application is made to them.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify. Jesus does not say who the father is. Jesus does not say what the vineyard is. Jesus does not say who the first son is. Jesus does not say who the second son is. Jesus does not say what “the will of his father” is beyond the command in the story. He does not say that either son stands for either group. He sets the two groups beside the answer, and the text does not go beyond that.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the story', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['The man (the father)', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 21:28'],
    ['The vineyard', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 21:28'],
    ['The first son', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 21:28–29'],
    ['The second son', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 21:30'],
    ['The work', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 21:28'],
    ['“The will of his father”', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 21:31'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Jesus compares two groups with the answer to His question, and He does not match each son to a group. A reader who says that the father is a certain person, that the vineyard is a certain people or that the first and second sons are the publicans and the chief priests is supplying what Jesus did not. The vineyard of 21:33 belongs to a different story and is not carried back into 21:28.'))
A(('p', '4.5 What the text does say. The text says that a man commanded two sons to work, that the first refused and afterward repented and went, that the second agreed and did not go, and that the first is the one who did the will of his father. It says that the publicans and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before the chief priests and elders, and it gives Jesus’ reason: John came in the way of righteousness, and they believed him, and the others did not believe him and did not repent afterward.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'Jesus gives an application, and it names two groups. This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison between the story and that application, and it assigns no identity to the father, the vineyard or either son.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. The story sets what a son says against what he does. Jesus asks which of the two did the will of the father, and the men who hear Him say the one who went. He then compares two groups of people with that answer: those who believed John and those who did not.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The deed answers the question.** Jesus asks who “did the will of his father,” and the answer rests on who went, not on who spoke the better word.',
    '**A refusal is not the last word.** The first son says “I will not,” and he “afterward” repented and went. The story counts his going.',
    '**Agreement is not the same as going.** The second son says “I go, sir,” and “went not.” His word was fair and the deed did not follow.',
    '**The hearers judge the case themselves.** Jesus does not announce the answer. He asks, and they say “The first.” They give the verdict on the story before He applies it.',
    '**The application turns on John.** What Jesus sets before the chief priests and elders is what they did with John: they “believed him not,” and they did not repent afterward, while the publicans and the harlots “believed him.”',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The chief priests and elders had asked by what authority Jesus acted, and they could not answer about John. The parable puts a question to them in the form of a story and then tells them what the question shows about their own response to John. They were meant to see that the publicans and the harlots, whom they would not have counted first, were going into the kingdom of God before them.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is the reversal of order. Those who gave the right answer in word, and sat in the temple, are said to follow those who had refused and then believed. Jesus does not say that the order is closed to them, and He does not say that it will not be. He says what the order is: “before you.”'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The will of the father is done in the going. A first refusal can be followed by repentance and going. Agreement in word is not the same as doing. John’s way of righteousness was believed by some and not by others. These statements arise from the story and from 21:31–32, and they do not say who the father, the vineyard or either son is.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say that the father is God or that the vineyard is Israel. It does not say that either son is the publicans, the harlots, the chief priests or the elders. It does not say when the publicans and the harlots enter the kingdom of God or what that entering is. It does not turn repenting or believing into steps by which a person is saved. It does not draw a meaning from another parable into this one.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The Two Sons stands among the parables Jesus spoke in the temple in the last week. It is kept apart here from the parables that follow it and from other stories of sons.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 21:33–46', '“a certain householder, which planted a vineyard”', 'The Wicked Tenants. It follows at once with “Hear another parable” (21:33). It has a vineyard, but a different owner, different workers and a different ending. Its vineyard is not carried back into the Two Sons.'],
    ['Matt 22:1–14', '“a certain king, which made a marriage for his son”', 'The Wedding Banquet. A king, a feast and invited guests. There is no command to work, no refusal and no going.'],
    ['Luke 15:11–32', '“A certain man had two sons”', 'The Lost Son and the Elder Brother. The opening words resemble Matthew 21:28, but the setting, the sons and the story differ. Neither is an explanation of the other.'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The Two Sons is told in the temple to the chief priests and the elders, immediately after their question about authority (21:23). The Lost Son is told to publicans, sinners, Pharisees and scribes in the setting of Luke 15:1–2. A shared phrase does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The Two Sons has a man, a first son and a second son. The Wicked Tenants has a householder, husbandmen and servants. The Lost Son has a father and two sons of its own. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The Two Sons moves from command to two answers to two deeds, and ends with a question and its application. The Wicked Tenants moves through a letting out and the sending of servants. The Wedding Banquet moves through an invitation and a refusal to come. None of those movements is read into this one.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the vineyard of 21:28 is not the vineyard of 21:33. Second, the first and second sons of Matthew 21 are not the two sons of Luke 15. Third, Jesus’ explanation in 21:31–32 is not an explanation of the stories that follow it.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The Two Sons is told to the chief priests and the elders of the people, in the temple, in answer to their question about His authority (21:23). It belongs to that ministry to Israel.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the Body of Christ were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The kingdom of God into which the publicans and the harlots go (21:31) is spoken of in Jesus’ ministry to Israel. It is not described in this passage as the Body of Christ, and the passage does not say that it is.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. Repenting and believing in this passage are what the first son did and what John’s hearers were called to in the Kingdom message to Israel (3:2; 4:17). They are not steps by which the Body of Christ is saved, and requirements that belong to that ministry are not laid on the Body. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). The first son’s going is a deed in a story, and it is not a work by which anyone is saved.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from the contrast the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of doing.** The story asks a reader to compare what he says with what he does, and to ask whether his deeds match his words.',
    '**A lesson of changing course.** The first son refuses and then goes. A reader learns that a wrong answer already given does not forbid a right deed afterward.',
    '**A lesson of honesty.** The second son’s “I go, sir” is a fair word without a deed. A reader learns to distrust his own fair words when they are not followed.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** The hearers give the verdict themselves, and the verdict falls on their own response. A reader learns to let a plain question show where he stands.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The chief priests, the elders, the publicans and the harlots are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and what was asked of John’s hearers is not turned into a requirement for the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'A man tells his first son to work in his vineyard that day. The son says “I will not,” afterward repents and goes. The man tells the second son the same, and he says “I go, sir” and does not go. Jesus asks which of them did the will of his father, and the chief priests and elders answer, “The first.” Jesus then tells them that the publicans and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before them, because John came in the way of righteousness and the publicans and the harlots believed him, while they did not believe him and did not repent afterward. Jesus does not name the father, the vineyard or either son. The comparison says that the will of the father is done in the going and not in the saying. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that names the father, the vineyard or the sons, or that makes repenting and believing here the way the Body is saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a command to work, a refusal followed by repentance and going, an agreement followed by no going, a question, an answer and an application.',
    '**The meaning:** the father’s will is done in the going; a first refusal can be followed by repentance; agreement in word is not doing.',
    '**The boundaries:** Jesus names no identity for the father, the vineyard or the sons; He compares two groups with the answer; the vineyard of 21:33 is not the vineyard of 21:28.',
    '**The present lesson:** doing, changing course, honesty and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

